---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SmithManoeuvreCalculator from '../../components/calculators/SmithManoeuvreCalculator.astro';

const title = 'Smith Manoeuvre Model';
const description = 'A case study in leveraged-investing scenario design, baseline fairness, formula auditing, and test-driven validation, with an educational Smith Manoeuvre calculator.';
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonicalPath="/work/smith-manoeuvre">
  <article class="smith-page">
    <header class="smith-hero page-shell">
      <div class="smith-hero-copy">
        <p class="eyebrow">06 · Decision support</p>
        <h1>{title}</h1>
        <p class="hero-dek">
          I built a leveraged-investing scenario model, then audited the parts most likely to make the comparison misleading:
          borrowing capacity, estimated tax-offset routing, cash symmetry, and payoff-period logic.
        </p>
        <p class="advice-badge">Educational scenario model — not financial, tax, legal, or lending advice.</p>
      </div>

      <dl class="proof-grid" aria-label="Project proof points">
        <div><dt>Model</dt><dd>Audited monthly V2</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Comparison</dt><dd>Equal-cash baseline</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Regression suite</dt><dd>21 / 21 scenarios pass</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Public artifact</dt><dd>Sanitized V2 workbook</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </header>

    <section class="case-section page-shell" aria-labelledby="modelling-problem">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="section-kicker">The modelling problem</p>
        <h2 id="modelling-problem">A simple comparison can become unfair surprisingly quickly.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="reading-copy">
        <p>
          The strategy combines mortgage amortization, changing revolving room, leveraged investing, borrowing cost,
          estimated tax treatment, and a counterfactual baseline. Each part changes over time, and a small accounting
          shortcut can compound into a large apparent difference over a long horizon.
        </p>
        <p>
          The first challenge was representing the strategy. The more important challenge became testing whether the
          alternative scenario was being given the same economic resources.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="mechanism" aria-labelledby="mechanism-title">
        <div class="mechanism-intro">
          <h3 id="mechanism-title">The public V2 mechanism</h3>
          <p>One monthly sequence, made explicit enough to test.</p>
        </div>
        <ol class="mechanism-flow">
          <li><span>01</span><strong>Mortgage payment</strong><small>reduces amortizing debt</small></li>
          <li><span>02</span><strong>Actual principal reduction</strong><small>measures what really disappeared</small></li>
          <li><span>03</span><strong>Eligible revolving room</strong><small>stays inside the simplified 65% envelope</small></li>
          <li><span>04</span><strong>Month-end HELOC draw</strong><small>becomes invested capital</small></li>
          <li><span>05</span><strong>Taxable portfolio</strong><small>grows under the entered return assumption</small></li>
          <li><span>06</span><strong>HELOC interest</strong><small>is paid from household cash on the opening balance</small></li>
          <li><span>07</span><strong>Estimated tax offset</strong><small>settles once per 12 model months</small></li>
        </ol>
        <p class="mechanism-note">
          The non-Smith baseline runs beside this sequence rather than being treated as an afterthought.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="fairness-section" aria-labelledby="fairness-title">
      <div class="page-shell fairness-inner">
        <div class="fairness-copy">
          <p class="section-kicker section-kicker-light">The centerpiece</p>
          <h2 id="fairness-title">The harder problem was making the comparison itself fair.</h2>
          <p>
            A strategy comparison is not fair if money used in one scenario simply disappears from the alternative.
            V2 turns that principle into a period-by-period accounting identity.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="cash-identity" aria-label="Equal external household cash comparison">
          <div class="cash-path">
            <p class="cash-label">Smith household cash</p>
            <p class="cash-equation">Fixed mortgage cash budget <span>+</span> HELOC interest</p>
          </div>
          <div class="cash-divider" aria-hidden="true">=</div>
          <div class="cash-path">
            <p class="cash-label">Baseline household cash</p>
            <p class="cash-equation">Fixed mortgage cash budget <span>+</span> matching investment equal to HELOC interest</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p class="fairness-footnote">
          If either mortgage needs less than the fixed monthly budget in its final payment period, the unused cash is
          invested in that scenario rather than disappearing. HELOC interest is therefore already represented in the
          comparison and is not subtracted a second time from the Smith ending position.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="case-section page-shell" aria-labelledby="audit-title">
      <div class="section-heading split-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="section-kicker">What the audit changed</p>
          <h2 id="audit-title">I treated plausible-looking outputs as something to challenge, not trust.</h2>
        </div>
        <p>
          The correction log became more useful than any single headline result because it exposed where model structure,
          rather than the strategy itself, could create an apparent advantage.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="audit-grid">
        <article>
          <p class="audit-number">01</p>
          <h3>Baseline symmetry</h3>
          <p>Starting resources and recurring household cash needed productive treatment on both sides of the comparison.</p>
          <p class="audit-check">Validation: strategy-disabled paths converge.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <p class="audit-number">02</p>
          <h3>Offset existence vs. destination</h3>
          <p>An estimated tax offset can change destination without ceasing to exist. V2 routes it to the mortgage first, then the portfolio after payoff.</p>
          <p class="audit-check">Validation: post-payoff offset remains in the model.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <p class="audit-number">03</p>
          <h3>Final-payoff readvancement</h3>
          <p>The private workbook could create room from scheduled principal beyond the debt actually repaid in the final period.</p>
          <p class="audit-check">V2 fix: new room uses opening mortgage minus closing mortgage.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <p class="audit-number">04</p>
          <h3>Conservative revolving boundary</h3>
          <p>The richer private model used an above-65% proxy. The public V2 does not create new revolving borrowing above a 65% total secured-LTV envelope.</p>
          <p class="audit-check">Validation: every positive draw respects the cap.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="case-section page-shell" aria-labelledby="private-public-title">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="section-kicker">From spreadsheet to public model</p>
        <h2 id="private-public-title">The browser tool is deliberately simpler than the private workbook.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="comparison-grid">
        <article class="comparison-panel">
          <p class="panel-label">Private spreadsheet</p>
          <h3>Richer modelling evidence</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>26-period biweekly architecture</li>
            <li>registered-account waterfalls and routing switches</li>
            <li>additional lender and portfolio proxies</li>
            <li>correction log and formula-audit history</li>
          </ul>
          <p>Useful for showing modelling depth, but not appropriate to transplant directly into a public consumer-facing tool.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="comparison-panel comparison-panel-accent">
          <p class="panel-label">Public calculator V2</p>
          <h3>Smaller, explicit, testable contract</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>monthly periods and constant home value</li>
            <li>no starting HELOC or interest capitalization</li>
            <li>no registered-account waterfall</li>
            <li>65% revolving envelope and equal-cash baseline</li>
          </ul>
          <p>The TypeScript engine and canonical regression fixtures are the implementation source of truth.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="calculator-section" aria-labelledby="calculator-title">
      <div class="page-shell calculator-intro">
        <p class="section-kicker">Interactive model</p>
        <h2 id="calculator-title">Try the audited public scenario model.</h2>
        <p>
          Change the assumptions and inspect the resulting debt, portfolio, payoff, LTV, and net-position paths. The
          calculator is educational and deterministic: it illustrates how assumptions interact, not what will happen or
          what anyone should do.
        </p>
      </div>
      <SmithManoeuvreCalculator />
    </section>

    <section class="case-section page-shell" aria-labelledby="rules-title">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="section-kicker">Accounting rules made visible</p>
        <h2 id="rules-title">The public contract removes ambiguity before the UI ever renders a result.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="rules-grid">
        <article>
          <h3>Rates and timing</h3>
          <p>
            The quoted mortgage rate follows the Canadian nominal semi-annual convention and is converted to a monthly
            equivalent. HELOC interest uses the annual rate divided by 12 on the opening monthly balance. Expected return
            is entered as an effective annual rate and converted to an equivalent monthly return.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Readvancement</h3>
          <p>
            New borrowing is based on actual mortgage principal reduction, not scheduled principal. The draw occurs at
            month end, so it begins incurring modelled HELOC interest in the following month.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Revolving room</h3>
          <p>
            The public model holds home value constant and creates no new revolving borrowing above a simplified 65%
            total secured-LTV envelope. An 80% boundary is used only to validate the starting mortgage example.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Estimated tax offset</h3>
          <p>
            Once every 12 model months, the model applies the entered deductible-interest assumption and marginal-rate
            proxy to that model year's HELOC interest. The offset goes to the mortgage first; any excess after payoff is invested.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="qa-section" aria-labelledby="qa-title">
      <div class="page-shell qa-layout">
        <div class="qa-stat" aria-label="21 of 21 canonical regression scenarios passing">
          <span>21 / 21</span>
          <p>canonical regression scenarios pass</p>
        </div>
        <div class="qa-copy">
          <p class="section-kicker">Validation</p>
          <h2 id="qa-title">The model has to satisfy invariants, not just produce a number.</h2>
          <ul class="qa-list">
            <li>Smith and baseline external household cash reconcile every month and cumulatively.</li>
            <li>A new HELOC draw cannot exceed actual mortgage principal reduction.</li>
            <li>A positive draw cannot breach the 65% secured-LTV envelope.</li>
            <li>Mortgage and HELOC balances cannot become negative.</li>
            <li>When readvancement disappears, matched scenarios converge.</li>
            <li>Post-payoff mortgage cash and estimated tax offsets stay productive rather than vanishing.</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="qa-note">
            The separate browser suite also checks field-to-engine mapping, edge cases, chart/table parity, keyboard use,
            privacy behavior, reduced motion, and narrow-screen containment.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="case-section page-shell" aria-labelledby="limits-title">
      <div class="section-heading split-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="section-kicker">Limits and assumptions</p>
          <h2 id="limits-title">A transparent model should show what it leaves out.</h2>
        </div>
        <p>
          These simplifications are part of the model boundary, not fine print. Real lender products, tax treatment,
          returns, rates, and cash-flow timing can differ materially.
        </p>
      </div>

      <ul class="limits-grid">
        <li>Monthly public model rather than the private workbook's biweekly engine</li>
        <li>Constant home value and constant mortgage / HELOC rates within a run</li>
        <li>Constant deterministic investment return; no volatility or sequence risk simulation</li>
        <li>Simplified HELOC interest timing rather than a lender statement engine</li>
        <li>Estimated tax proxy only; the calculator does not determine deductibility</li>
        <li>No fees, refinancing costs, investment-tax engine, or registered-account contribution-room model</li>
        <li>No lender-specific above-65% amortizing tranche or promise of available credit</li>
        <li>Borrowing to invest increases exposure to both market losses and borrowing-cost changes</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="disclosure-grid">
        <div>
          <h3>Tax</h3>
          <p>
            The estimated tax offset is only an illustration. Interest deductibility depends on the actual use and
            tracing of borrowed funds, income-earning purpose, legal obligations, record keeping, and other tax rules.
            This calculator does not determine tax eligibility.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Lending</h3>
          <p>
            This educational model uses simplified 65% revolving and 80% starting-LTV boundaries. Actual HELOC limits,
            combined-loan rules, authorized limits, underwriting, payments, and readvancement behavior depend on the
            lender and current requirements.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Mortgage mechanics</h3>
          <p>
            The mortgage calculation is a standardized monthly illustration. Actual payment timing, prepayment
            privileges, variable-rate mechanics, fees, and lender calculations may differ.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Investment risk</h3>
          <p>
            Borrowing to invest increases exposure to both market losses and interest-rate changes. Returns are
            uncertain, borrowing costs can rise, and investments can underperform borrowing costs for long periods.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="workbook-section" aria-labelledby="workbook-title">
      <div class="page-shell workbook-inner">
        <div>
          <p class="section-kicker">Public companion</p>
          <h2 id="workbook-title">Sanitized V2 demonstration workbook</h2>
          <p>
            Hypothetical inputs only. This spreadsheet companion follows the public calculator's monthly V2 formula
            architecture, while the TypeScript reference model and regression suite remain the implementation source of truth.
          </p>
          <p class="workbook-disclaimer">Educational only; not financial, tax, legal, or lending advice.</p>
        </div>
        <a class="download-link" href="/downloads/Smith_Manoeuvre_Public_Sanitized_V2.xlsx" download>
          Download the sanitized V2 demonstration workbook
          <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="learning-section page-shell" aria-labelledby="learning-title">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="section-kicker">What this project demonstrates</p>
        <h2 id="learning-title">The useful skill was turning assumptions into rules that could fail visibly.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="learning-grid">
        <p>
          I translated a complicated household-finance question into a formula-driven model, documented the timing and
          cash-routing assumptions, and then built tests around the places where a comparison could create value through
          accounting rather than through the modelled mechanism.
        </p>
        <p>
          The result is less about producing a persuasive forecast and more about model design: defining a fair
          counterfactual, isolating simplifications, finding edge-case defects, and carrying the same accounting contract
          from spreadsheet evidence into a public TypeScript implementation.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .smith-page {
    overflow: clip;
  }

  .page-shell {
    width: min(calc(100% - 2.25rem), var(--content-main));
    margin-inline: auto;
  }

  .smith-hero {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.4fr) minmax(18rem, 0.6fr);
    gap: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 6.5rem);
    align-items: end;
    padding-block: clamp(3.75rem, 8vw, 7.5rem) clamp(4.5rem, 8vw, 7rem);
  }

  .eyebrow,
  .section-kicker,
  .panel-label,
  .cash-label,
  .audit-number {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 0.76rem;
    font-weight: 760;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    line-height: 1.2;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .eyebrow,
  .section-kicker,
  .panel-label,
  .audit-number {
    color: var(--color-teal);
  }

  .smith-hero h1 {
    max-width: 58rem;
    margin: 0.7rem 0 0;
    font-size: clamp(3.35rem, 7vw, 6.6rem);
    line-height: 0.94;
    letter-spacing: -0.065em;
  }

  .hero-dek {
    max-width: 50rem;
    margin: 1.5rem 0 0;
    color: var(--color-text);
    font-size: clamp(1.08rem, 1.8vw, 1.35rem);
    line-height: 1.54;
  }

  .advice-badge {
    display: inline-block;
    max-width: 44rem;
    margin: 1.35rem 0 0;
    padding: 0.72rem 0.9rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-line-strong);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    background: var(--color-surface-warm);
    color: var(--color-ink);
    font-size: 0.86rem;
    font-weight: 690;
    line-height: 1.4;
  }

  .proof-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    margin: 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-line-strong);
  }

  .proof-grid div {
    min-width: 0;
    padding: 1rem 0.4rem 1.05rem 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-line-strong);
  }

  .proof-grid div:nth-child(odd) {
    padding-right: 1rem;
    border-right: 1px solid var(--color-line-strong);
  }

  .proof-grid div:nth-child(even) {
    padding-left: 1rem;
  }

  .proof-grid dt {
    color: var(--color-muted);
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    font-weight: 720;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .proof-grid dd {
    margin: 0.38rem 0 0;
    color: var(--color-ink);
    font-size: 0.96rem;
    font-weight: 710;
    line-height: 1.35;
  }

  .case-section {
    padding-block: clamp(4.5rem, 8vw, 7.5rem);
  }

  .section-heading {
    max-width: 58rem;
  }

  .section-heading h2,
  .fairness-copy h2,
  .calculator-intro h2,
  .qa-copy h2,
  .workbook-inner h2 {
    margin: 0.65rem 0 0;
    color: var(--color-ink);
    font-size: clamp(2.2rem, 4.2vw, 4rem);
    line-height: 1.02;
    letter-spacing: -0.045em;
  }

  .reading-copy {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: clamp(1.5rem, 4vw, 4rem);
    max-width: 69rem;
    margin-top: 2rem;
  }

  .reading-copy p,
  .split-heading > p,
  .learning-grid p {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--color-text);
    font-size: clamp(1rem, 1.25vw, 1.12rem);
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .mechanism {
    margin-top: clamp(3rem, 6vw, 5rem);
    padding: clamp(1.5rem, 3vw, 2.4rem);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-line-strong);
    border-radius: var(--radius-card);
    background: var(--color-surface);
  }

  .mechanism-intro {
